.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/any-promise Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Any Promise"
.PP
\fR\fP
.PP
Let your library support any ES 2015 (ES6) compatible \fRPromise\fP and leave the choice to application authors\&. The application can \fIoptionally\fP register its preferred \fRPromise\fP implementation and it will be exported when requiring \fRany-promise\fP from library code\&.

.PP
If no preference is registered, defaults to the global \fRPromise\fP for newer Node\&.js versions\&. The browser version defaults to the window \fRPromise\fP, so polyfill or register as necessary\&.
.SS "Usage with global Promise:"
Assuming the global \fRPromise\fP is the desired implementation:

.PP
.PP
.nf
# Install any libraries depending on any\-promise
$ npm install mz
.fi
.PP

.PP
The installed libraries will use global Promise by default\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// in library
var Promise = require('any\-promise')  // the global Promise

function promiseReturningFunction(){
    return new Promise(function(resolve, reject){\&.\&.\&.})
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Usage with registration:"
Assuming \fRbluebird\fP is the desired Promise implementation:

.PP
.PP
.nf
# Install preferred promise library
$ npm install bluebird
# Install any\-promise to allow registration
$ npm install any\-promise
# Install any libraries you would like to use depending on any\-promise
$ npm install mz
.fi
.PP

.PP
Register your preference in the application entry point before any other \fRrequire\fP of packages that load \fRany-promise\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// top of application index\&.js or other entry point
require('any\-promise/register/bluebird')

// \-or\- Equivalent to above, but allows customization of Promise library
require('any\-promise/register')('bluebird', {Promise: require('bluebird')})
.fi
.PP

.PP
Now that the implementation is registered, you can use any package depending on \fRany-promise\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var fsp = require('mz/fs') // mz/fs will use registered bluebird promises
var Promise = require('any\-promise')  // the registered bluebird promise 
.fi
.PP

.PP
It is safe to call \fRregister\fP multiple times, but it must always be with the same implementation\&.

.PP
Again, registration is \fIoptional\fP\&. It should only be called by the application user if overriding the global \fRPromise\fP implementation is desired\&.
.SS "Optional Application Registration"
As an application author, you can \fIoptionally\fP register a preferred \fRPromise\fP implementation on application startup (before any call to \fRrequire('any-promise')\fP:

.PP
You must register your preference before any call to \fRrequire('any-promise')\fP (by you or required packages), and only one implementation can be registered\&. Typically, this registration would occur at the top of the application entry point\&.
.SS "Registration shortcuts"
If you are using a known \fRPromise\fP implementation, you can register your preference with a shortcut:

.PP
.PP
.nf
require('any\-promise/register/bluebird')
// \-or\-
import 'any\-promise/register/q';
.fi
.PP

.PP
Shortcut registration is the preferred registration method as it works in the browser and Node\&.js\&. It is also convenient for using with \fRimport\fP and many test runners, that offer a \fR--require\fP flag:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ ava \-\-require=any\-promise/register/bluebird test\&.js
.fi
.PP

.PP
Current known implementations include \fRbluebird\fP, \fRq\fP, \fRwhen\fP, \fRrsvp\fP, \fRes6-promise\fP, \fRpromise\fP, \fRnative-promise-only\fP, \fRpinkie\fP, \fRvow\fP and \fRlie\fP\&. If you are not using a known implementation, you can use another registration method described below\&.
.SS "Basic Registration"
As an alternative to registration shortcuts, you can call the \fRregister\fP function with the preferred \fRPromise\fP implementation\&. The benefit of this approach is that a \fRPromise\fP library can be required by name without being a known implementation\&. This approach does NOT work in the browser\&. To use \fRany-promise\fP in the browser use either registration shortcuts or specify the \fRPromise\fP constructor using advanced registration (see below)\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
require('any\-promise/register')('when')
// \-or\- require('any\-promise/register')('any other ES6 compatible library (known or otherwise)')
.fi
.PP

.PP
This registration method will try to detect the \fRPromise\fP constructor from requiring the specified implementation\&. If you would like to specify your own constructor, see advanced registration\&.
.SS "Advanced Registration"
To use the browser version, you should either install a polyfill or explicitly register the \fRPromise\fP constructor:

.PP
.PP
.nf
require('any\-promise/register')('bluebird', {Promise: require('bluebird')})
.fi
.PP

.PP
This could also be used for registering a custom \fRPromise\fP implementation or subclass\&.

.PP
Your preference will be registered globally, allowing a single registration even if multiple versions of \fRany-promise\fP are installed in the NPM dependency tree or are using multiple bundled JavaScript files in the browser\&. You can bypass this global registration in options:

.PP
.PP
.nf
require('\&.\&./register')('es6\-promise', {Promise: require('es6\-promise')\&.Promise, global: false})
.fi
.PP
.SS "Library Usage"
To use any \fRPromise\fP constructor, simply require it:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var Promise = require('any\-promise');

return Promise
  \&.all([xf, f, init, coll])
  \&.then(fn);


return new Promise(function(resolve, reject){
  try {
    resolve(item);
  } catch(e){
    reject(e);
  }
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
Except noted below, libraries using \fRany-promise\fP should only use \fRdocumented\fP functions as there is no guarantee which implementation will be chosen by the application author\&. Libraries should never call \fRregister\fP, only the application user should call if desired\&.
.SS "Advanced Library Usage"
If your library needs to branch code based on the registered implementation, you can retrieve it using \fRvar impl = require('any-promise/implementation')\fP, where \fRimpl\fP will be the package name (\fR"bluebird"\fP, \fR"when"\fP, etc\&.) if registered, \fR"global\&.Promise"\fP if using the global version on Node\&.js, or \fR"window\&.Promise"\fP if using the browser version\&. You should always include a default case, as there is no guarantee what package may be registered\&.
.SS "Support for old Node\&.js versions"
Node\&.js versions prior to \fRv0\&.12\fP may have contained buggy versions of the global \fRPromise\fP\&. For this reason, the global \fRPromise\fP is not loaded automatically for these old versions\&. If using \fRany-promise\fP in Node\&.js versions versions \fR<= v0\&.12\fP, the user should register a desired implementation\&.

.PP
If an implementation is not registered, \fRany-promise\fP will attempt to discover an installed \fRPromise\fP implementation\&. If no implementation can be found, an error will be thrown on \fRrequire('any-promise')\fP\&. While the auto-discovery usually avoids errors, it is non-deterministic\&. It is recommended that the user always register a preferred implementation for older Node\&.js versions\&.

.PP
This auto-discovery is only available for Node\&.jS versions prior to \fRv0\&.12\fP\&. Any newer versions will always default to the global \fRPromise\fP implementation\&.
.SS "Related"
